A||||4||Call||Questions on 84 Fieros||||5||
Z||000000||Call||12-29-1999||02:35 AM||||Ok, just gathering information, is it wise to stay away from 84 Fieros and just buy 85s' and above? I've heard there were alot of problems with the 84s' and that there was a chance of a fire in the engine compartment, is this true?||||reg||5
Z||000001||Voyd||12-29-1999||02:53 AM||||theyve all been recalled for that engine fire thing. in general the 84's are the most beat up and neglected and often in really bad shape. .also they only have the (weak) 4cyl motor. in my case i test drove a 4cyl then tried the v6 and that was that, once u taste v6 youre hooked.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Monkeyman||12-29-1999||03:18 AM||||There's nothing wrong with '84s, but if you really want a V6 (even in the future) go for an '85 or newer.  Ed Parks (of the Fiero Factory) said that it's a real bi*** to put a later model motor into an '84.  You can get better deals on alot of '84s.||||reg||1
Z||000003||ChadMan||12-29-1999||10:22 AM||||I think later Fieros tend to be more reliable than earlier ones.  It seemed like the first year I owned my 84, it was always giving me some sort of electrical problem (sensors, fusible links, etc).  But after I got it debugged, it was a very reliable car for several years.  Plus it had the 4-speed with the 4.10 gear - it was a lot quicker off the line than my 88.<P>And Voyd's right - Pontiac recalled all the 84's and offered to install new engines in them because of a con-rod problem, so very few still have the original problematic engines.<P>I wouldn't rule out the 84's - you can find some nice ones cheap.  But if you can find a newer Fiero in similar condition in the same price range, go with the newer one.||||reg||1
Z||000004||fierosound||12-29-1999||12:43 PM||||General rule of thumb...<P>NEVER BUY THE FIRST YEAR A NEW CAR COMES OUT!!<P>The first year is always the one loaded with problems, the second year is better, and by the third, the car starts to get pretty good.  This seems to apply to all cars made in North America.  I think all too often, the manufacturers "rush" the car into production without solving all the problems first.<P>On second thought, it seems computer software manufacturers do the same thing....||||reg||10
